## Proof

We claim that such UFDs **do exist**. We exhibit an explicit example and verify all the required properties.

### The Example

Fix a prime number $p$. Define
$$R = \mathbb{Z}_{(p)} = \left\{\,\frac{a}{b}\in\mathbb{Q} : a,b\in\mathbb{Z},\; p\nmid b\,\right\}.$$
This is the localization of $\mathbb{Z}$ at the prime ideal $(p)$.

### $R$ is a UFD

$R$ is a localization of the PID $\mathbb{Z}$, hence $R$ is a PID (every ideal of $R$ is the localization of an ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$, hence principal). Every PID is a UFD, so $R$ is a UFD.

### $R$ is not a field

The element $p\in R$ is not a unit: its inverse $1/p$ does not lie in $R$, since $p\mid p$ violates the defining condition $p\nmid b$. Hence $R$ has a non-unit, so $R$ is not a field.

### $R$ has exactly one prime element up to associativity

We analyze the structure of $R$. Take any nonzero element $a/b\in R$ with $p\nmid b$. Factor the integer $a$ as $a = p^{n}\cdot a'$ with $n\ge 0$ and $p\nmid a'$. Then
$$\frac{a}{b} = \frac{a'}{b}\cdot p^{n}.$$
Since $p\nmid a'$ and $p\nmid b$, the fraction $a'/b$ is a unit of $R$: its inverse $b/a'$ lies in $R$ because $p\nmid a'$. Therefore **every nonzero element of $R$ is of the form $u\cdot p^{n}$** where $u$ is a unit and $n\ge 0$.

Now we determine the prime (= irreducible, since $R$ is a UFD) elements:
- An element $u\cdot p^{n}$ with $n=0$ is a unit, hence not prime.
- An element $u\cdot p^{n}$ with $n\ge 2$ factors as $(u\cdot p)\cdot p^{n-1}$, a product of two non-units, hence is not irreducible, hence not prime.
- An element $u\cdot p$ with $n=1$ is irreducible: if $u\cdot p = xy$ with $x,y$ non-units, write $x = v\cdot p^{k}$, $y = w\cdot p^{\ell}$ with $k,\ell\ge 1$ (non-unit forces exponent $\ge 1$); then $vw\cdot p^{k+\ell} = u\cdot p$, giving $k+\ell = 1$, contradicting $k,\ell\ge 1$.

Thus the irreducible elements are exactly the elements $u\cdot p$ with $u$ a unit, i.e., precisely the **associates of $p$**. Since $R$ is a UFD, irreducible elements are prime, so the prime elements are exactly the associates of $p$.

Consequently, $R$ has **exactly one prime element up to associativity**, namely $p$.

### Conclusion

$R = \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is a UFD (being a PID), is not a field (since $p$ is a non-unit), and has only finitely many — in fact exactly one — prime element up to associativity. Therefore, **there exist UFDs with only finitely many prime elements up to associativity that are not fields**. $\blacksquare$

